<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Poitou</h1><p class="st-meta">778 CE – 1416 CE (approx.) · capital Poitiers</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">County centred on Poitiers whose counts became dukes of Aquitaine and passed their lands to Eleanor of Aquitaine and the Plantagenets.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Carolingian counts ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> from the late 8th century. In the 9th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramnulfids">Ramnulfid</a> family made the office hereditary, and in the 10th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iii-duke-of-aquitaine">Count William III</a>, called Towhead, took the title of Duke of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Aquitaine</a>, after which the two titles were held together.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> over a land of abbeys, castles and salt-producing coasts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ix-duke-of-aquitaine">Duke William IX</a>, the first known troubadour, wrote poetry in Occitan, and his granddaughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a> carried the county first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france">Louis VII</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II</a> seized Poitou from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> from 1204, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-viii-of-france">Louis VIII</a> completed the conquest in 1224. It became an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alphonse-count-of-poitiers">Alphonse of Poitiers</a> and later for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-duke-of-berry">John, Duke of Berry</a>, on whose death in 1416 it returned to the crown.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-poitou.jpg" alt="County of Poitou" width="320" height="382"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:France_1154-fr.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>778 CE – 1416 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Poitiers</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>William IX, Eleanor of Aquitaine, Alphonse of Poitiers</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French, Old Occitan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Poitou, County of Poitiers</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1097&amp;focus=county-of-poitou" aria-label="County of Poitou on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4688 540 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Poitou lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5008 796L5035 888M5008 796L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5035 888h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5008 796h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5008 796h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Poitiers. <a href="/atlas?year=1097&amp;focus=county-of-poitou">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
